Check for an interrupt in every function call.

libexpr calls checkInterrupt only while it prints a value: print.cc,
print-ambiguous.cc, value-to-json.cc and value-to-xml.cc. Pure evaluation
never checks, so a cancel cannot stop it (huggorm#158). Measured before
this patch: a cancelled call to

    let sum = builtins.foldl' (a: b: a + b) 0;
        range = n: builtins.genList (x: x) n;
    in builtins.foldl' (a: _: a + sum (range 1000)) 0 (range 1000000)

returned 67.68s after the cancel, and held its EvalState all that time.

Every loop in the language is a chain of function calls, so
EvalState::callFunction is the one place that sees each iteration.
EvalState::eval had this check until 2012 (f491ae97d), which removed it
for speed.

The cost, measured on 2.34.8 with huggorm's interrupt hook installed:
the expression above with range 100000 makes 300,300,001 calls, and
takes 7.71s before and 8.00s after, best of 15 runs each. That is about
1 ns, or 4%, per call, in an expression that does nothing but call.

The hunk has no signature line in its context, so the same file applies
to 2.34, 2.35 and git. On 2.34 it needs nix/util/signals.hh, which
nix-2.34-interrupted-thunk-recovers.patch includes.

--- a/src/libexpr/eval.cc
+++ b/src/libexpr/eval.cc
@@ -1511,6 +1511,10 @@
 {
     auto _level = addCallDepth(pos);

+    /* Every loop in the language is a chain of calls, so this makes a
+       pure evaluation interruptible. */
+    checkInterrupt();
+
     auto neededHooks = profiler.getNeededHooks();
     if (neededHooks.test(EvalProfiler::preFunctionCall)) [[unlikely]]
         profiler.preFunctionCallHook(*this, fun, args, pos);
